WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.357
Good evening, my name is Alan Bernstein
and I'm the President and CEO of CIFAR.

00:00:05.357 --> 00:00:11.007
Imagine that later this evening some
aliens are going to visit us here at the

00:00:11.007 --> 00:00:17.126
Artscape Daniel's launchpad from
another planet. Imagine that they

00:00:17.126 --> 00:00:21.747
immediately demand to know what is our
most proud achievement

00:00:21.747 --> 00:00:25.021
what are our proudest accomplishments?

00:00:25.021 --> 00:00:26.967
what would we say?

00:00:26.967 --> 00:00:30.807
Well of course being at an Arts Center, we
could cite the words of Bach, Stravinksi,

00:00:30.807 --> 00:00:35.877
the group of seven, Shakespeare, Picasso.
Yet would these beautiful works be

00:00:35.877 --> 00:00:41.457
understood or even appreciated by other
sentient beings with experiences and

00:00:41.457 --> 00:00:46.317
worlds unimaginably different from our
own? After all, even when Picasso started

00:00:46.317 --> 00:00:51.236
doing his cubist paintings, other humans
failed to appreciate his art. And when

00:00:51.236 --> 00:00:55.617
Stravinsky's Rite of Spring was first
performed, the audience fled from the hall.

00:00:55.617 --> 00:01:02.100
Why then, would we expect aliens to
appreciate our own artistic accomplishments?

00:01:02.100 --> 00:01:08.200
But there is one area of human accomplishment of which we can rightly boast.

00:01:08.200 --> 00:01:12.057
It is the one achievement
that reflects the unique power of the

00:01:12.057 --> 00:01:17.366
human brain and our curiosity about the
world around us. And it is why our

00:01:17.366 --> 00:01:23.697
species is called Homo sapiens, which
means wise humans. And that achievement,

00:01:23.697 --> 00:01:29.726
that uniquely human achievement, is
called science. Science has explained

00:01:29.726 --> 00:01:33.866
much about the history and the mystery
of the universe. The physical laws that

00:01:33.866 --> 00:01:38.936
govern all matter, the mechanisms of
heredity and soon, an understanding of

00:01:38.936 --> 00:01:43.737
how we think and feel. And the fruits of
science have transformed our

00:01:43.737 --> 00:01:47.209
world in myriads of ways. Consider
smallpox.

00:01:47.209 --> 00:01:52.400
Smallpox killed approximately 300 million people in the 20th century.

00:01:52.400 --> 00:02:00.500
In this century no one will die of smallpox. No one. That's science.

00:02:00.500 --> 00:02:02.937
The fruits of science have also doubled our

00:02:02.937 --> 00:02:06.150
lifespan as Canadians since
Confederation.

00:02:06.150 --> 00:02:08.900
Doubled our lifespans since Confederation.

00:02:08.900 --> 00:02:11.987
Science has made it
possible to light and heat our homes.

00:02:11.987 --> 00:02:18.580
Science has saved over 1 billion lives
due to vaccines and antibiotics in the 20th century.

00:02:18.580 --> 00:02:22.846
Science has provided
evidence-driven changes in social policy.

00:02:22.846 --> 00:02:27.627
Scientific research has been a catalyst
for equity-based social movements,

00:02:27.627 --> 00:02:30.417
including a woman's right to vote,

00:02:30.417 --> 00:02:35.340
education for all, and healthcare for all in many countries.

00:02:35.340 --> 00:02:39.147
We truly live in an age defined by, and transformed by science.

00:02:39.147 --> 00:02:42.157
That's why I have the best job
in Canada.

00:02:42.157 --> 00:02:45.000
In fact I think I have the best job in the world.

00:02:45.000 --> 00:02:48.737
As CIFAR's president and as a scientist,

00:02:48.737 --> 00:02:54.717
I oversee a global network of the world's
very top scientists, their collaborators,

00:02:54.717 --> 00:03:00.057
our partners, and our donors, who together are transforming our understanding of

00:03:00.057 --> 00:03:04.647
the biological, the physical, and the
social worlds in which we live.

00:03:04.647 --> 00:03:08.580
And in the process, are transforming our world.

00:03:08.580 --> 00:03:10.392
That's what I call impact.

00:03:10.392 --> 00:03:15.380
CIFAR convenes the world's top scientists to address
complex questions

00:03:15.380 --> 00:03:21.057
such as, how does our childhood experiences and/or our
socioeconomic status affect our

00:03:21.057 --> 00:03:23.680
trajectory of lifelong health?

00:03:23.680 --> 00:03:26.300
How do microbes that live in and on us, our microbiome,

00:03:26.300 --> 00:03:31.527
affect our health, human
evolution, and our behaviour?

00:03:31.527 --> 00:03:35.457
What can nature teach us about
harvesting and storing energy from the Sun?

00:03:35.457 --> 00:03:41.307
How can we understand the molecular
underpinnings of human intelligence?

00:03:41.307 --> 00:03:43.035
What is consciousness?

00:03:43.035 --> 00:03:47.906
Why are some societies
successful, and others not?

00:03:47.906 --> 00:03:52.676
These are all examples of CIFAR programs that are
going on at the very moment.

00:03:52.676 --> 00:03:54.480
But science is much, much more than that.

00:03:54.480 --> 00:03:57.466
Put simply, it is the best way that

00:03:57.466 --> 00:04:02.686
humanity has come up with to understand
and improve the world in which we live.

00:04:02.686 --> 00:04:08.200
Science creates revolutions and
revelations that echo right across generations.

00:04:08.200 --> 00:04:12.927
But scientific discoveries
have not only advanced our understanding

00:04:12.927 --> 00:04:18.057
of the world. They have lifted the veil
of ignorance, superstition, and terror

00:04:18.057 --> 00:04:22.399
that has characterized much of
humanity's experiences before science.

00:04:22.399 --> 00:04:27.000
Today science is addressing the great challenges our world is facing.

00:04:27.000 --> 00:04:30.377
Those challenges, to name
but a few, include terrorism, food and water security,

00:04:30.377 --> 00:04:35.417
mental health, infectious
disease - think of Covid 19 - and of course

00:04:35.417 --> 00:04:40.200
the climate crisis to name but a few are
global

00:04:40.200 --> 00:04:43.000
and they all require scientific research for their solution.

00:04:43.000 --> 00:04:45.000
and that's where CIFAR comes in.

00:04:45.000 --> 00:04:50.300
As a Canadian-based, global organization, we convene the world's top scientists

00:04:50.300 --> 00:04:53.000
400 of them, at the moment, from 23 countries

00:04:53.000 --> 00:04:56.687
who work together
across disciplines to address the

00:04:56.687 --> 00:05:01.900
solutions needed to address the most
important questions facing science and humanity.

00:05:01.900 --> 00:05:05.940
CIFAR was founded on a
disarmingly simple idea.

00:05:05.940 --> 00:05:09.100
The best way to address the important questions of our time

00:05:09.100 --> 00:05:10.967
is to bring together the best people,

00:05:10.967 --> 00:05:16.306
across disciplines and borders, to
engage in deep thinking and discussions

00:05:16.306 --> 00:05:21.046
about their solutions. We identify big,
complex, and challenging questions and

00:05:21.046 --> 00:05:25.576
then identify the very best scientists
from anywhere in the world to work on

00:05:25.576 --> 00:05:31.396
those questions. We create a safe space
where brilliant minds come together to

00:05:31.396 --> 00:05:35.776
form global communities, challenge each
other as colleagues,

00:05:35.776 --> 00:05:39.977
forge new collaborations across disciplines, that hopefully change the world's thinking

00:05:39.977 --> 00:05:44.717
about an important question. That simple
idea led to the current breakthrough in

00:05:44.717 --> 00:05:48.227
artificial intelligence, our understanding of the importance of the early years,

00:05:48.227 --> 00:05:52.127
and insights into the central role that strong

00:05:52.127 --> 00:05:55.820
institutions play in economic growth.

00:05:55.820 --> 00:05:59.000
Consider artificial intelligence for a moment - AI.

00:05:59.000 --> 00:06:02.446
About 20 years ago CIFAR
brought together a very small group of

00:06:02.446 --> 00:06:07.727
mathematicians, computer scientists,
neurophysiologists, and cognitive

00:06:07.727 --> 00:06:12.946
psychologists, with the goal to
understand how humans learn.

00:06:12.946 --> 00:06:17.746
Over the decades that followed, that CIFAR team developed new insights that resulted in

00:06:17.746 --> 00:06:22.200
deep learning and the revolution in AI
taking place around the world.

00:06:22.200 --> 00:06:25.200
Yes, AI is a Canadian breakthrough

00:06:25.200 --> 00:06:29.000
and we are all reaping the benefits of that "made in Canada" discovery

00:06:29.000 --> 00:06:32.056
through the high-tech explosion taking place here in Toronto,

00:06:32.056 --> 00:06:34.600
Montreal, and Edmonton and across
the country.

00:06:34.600 --> 00:06:37.500
And the CIFAR Azrieli Global Scholars Program,

00:06:37.500 --> 00:06:40.937
supported by the Azrieli Foundation and Nancy and John Love,

00:06:40.937 --> 00:06:45.317
identifies the world's most
promising young scientists and provides

00:06:45.317 --> 00:06:50.717
the funds and mentorship they need to
launch their successful careers.

00:06:50.717 --> 00:06:54.947
Because of science we live longer, we suffer less and we enjoy lives

00:06:54.947 --> 00:06:57.518
that were unimaginable even a hundred years ago.

00:06:57.518 --> 00:06:59.447
We have learned to advance and apply knowledge,

00:06:59.447 --> 00:07:04.907
robust knowledge, not acquired
by superstition or myth, but by science

00:07:04.907 --> 00:07:07.640
and evidence, to improve the human
condition.

00:07:07.640 --> 00:07:11.300
And that's why investments in science always has impact.

00:07:11.300 --> 00:07:14.026
Science is indeed the light that illuminates the

00:07:14.026 --> 00:07:18.827
darkness in the world by advancing our
understanding of the world.

00:07:18.827 --> 00:07:22.100
And science reflects the very best of our humanity.

00:07:22.100 --> 00:07:24.257
The use of our brain and our curiosity

00:07:24.257 --> 00:07:29.387
to understand the world around us.
It is this curiosity, this insistence on

00:07:29.387 --> 00:07:33.246
the challenging of dogma and belief, I
think, that would impress those

00:07:33.246 --> 00:07:37.417
extraterrestrial visitors that we're all
going to meet later on this evening.

00:07:37.417 --> 00:07:42.227
I encourage you to think about how science
has made an impact on your own life.

00:07:42.227 --> 00:07:46.097
From the last time you used your cell phone,
to flipping a switch to turn on a light

00:07:46.097 --> 00:07:51.137
to the flu vaccine that I hope you all
got, to a loved one who is alive because

00:07:51.137 --> 00:07:56.537
of scientific research. The donors who
support CIFAR are truly impressive because

00:07:56.537 --> 00:08:02.117
they understand that despite the long
timelines involved, the impact of science

00:08:02.117 --> 00:08:08.146
on our lives is staggering. Science has
truly changed our world.

00:08:08.146 --> 00:08:12.706
They understand that if you want impact, if you want to change the world, you can do no better

00:08:12.706 --> 00:08:16.100
than to invest in science and in CIFAR.

00:08:16.100 --> 00:08:18.396
Thank you for your attention.

